// A "go" or "defer" whose operand the parser cannot read is reported AT THE
// KEYWORD, not at the top of the file.
//
// Such an operand -- a function literal, which the grammar has no rule for --
// leaves a head holding no token of its own, and that head's position is the
// file's FIRST token. Every one of these used to be reported at line 1, column 1,
// which sends the reader to the wrong end of the program.
//
// The unparseable case comes last: it ends the parse, so nothing after it is
// checked -- and a name used after it reads as undefined, which is why there is
// nothing after it here. That one is reported by the PARSER, at the "func" it
// cannot read: a parse error outranks a checker error on the same line, because
// what the checker says about a line it could not parse describes a consequence
// where the parse error describes the cause. Here the checker's version --
// "defer statement must be a function call" -- was wrong as well as second: the
// operand is a call, and what the grammar has no rule for is the literal.

var x int

func main() {
	// ERROR defer statement must be a function call
	defer x
	// ERROR go statement must be a function call
	go x
	// ERROR "func" \["func"\]: expected \[identifier '\*' '\('\]
	defer func() {
	}()
}
